# WorkflowHub

Downloads Galaxy workflows from WorkflowHub and finds which tool connnects to which ande translates the tools to bio.tools IDs to compare with the literature graph.

The scripts to run:
```
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/download_workflowhub.py     # Downloads .ga files from Workflowhub
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/galaxy_tool_pairs.py        # Extracts the .ga workflow nodes and edges
.venv/bin/python directed-graph/workflowhub/map_galaxy_tools.py         # Translates/mapps the .ga node IDs/names to bio.tools IDs/names
```

`download_workflowhub.py` gets the metadata of every workflow on WorkflowHub, then downloads the RO-Crate (zip) of each Galaxy workflow and takes out its `.ga` file. Files already downloaded are skipped, so it can be stopped and run again. Add `--limit 5` to test on a few. 

`galaxy_tool_pairs.py` extracts the edges from the workflow. Tools are named by their ToolShed repository (e.g. "megahit"), not by bio.tools ID.
Steps that are not real tools such as inputs, and helper tools are skipped, and the "real" tools on each side are connected through them.
Did spot check n = 5 workflows, comparing them to the visual graph in workflowhub (e.g. https://workflowhub.org/workflows/4?version=1 and edges in directed-graph/workflowhub/data/galaxy/galaxy_pairs.csv with workflow_id == 4). 
It produces three files:
- data/galaxy/galaxy_pairs.csv  one row per workflow and tool pair: tool A feeds tool B
- data/galaxy/galaxy_edges.csv  one row per tool pair, summed over workflows
- data/galaxy/galaxy_tools.csv  one row per tool, and how many workflows use it

*Note: Each tool pair is counted once per workflow, so if the same pair appears on several branches of one workflow, it is listed once.*
*We might want TODO something to address that later.* 

`map_galaxy_tools.py` maps galaxy IDs to bio.tool IDs using the galaxy codex (add https://github.com/galaxyproject/galaxy_codex/blob/main/communities/all/resources/tools.tsv to the data/mapping/ folder under the name galaxy_tool_map.csv). If not in the codex, it tried to match the name to a bio.tools ID (directed-graph/biotools/data/biotools_raw_14-10-2026.jsonl). Third, if no match is found, the suffix is removed and name match is attempted again (to find user annotations liek `samtools_view` -> `samtools`). 
It produces four files:
- data/mapping/galaxy_tool_map.csv  the translation map, has attribute "method" to be used to distinguish what grouping we trust and not
- data/biotools/biotools_pairs.csv  one row per workflow and tool pair
- data/biotools/biotools_edges.csv  one row per pair, summed over workflows:
- data/biotools/biotools_nodes.csv  one row per bio.tools id, how many workflows use it

*Note: Prefix method might be a bit weak but printing top 10 most found prexies seems to suggest it works ok. I added a blocklist for some false matches.*
*The data should probably be grouped by confidence in match; codex > name match > prefix name match*

This is the full repo structure 

```
data/
├── download/                   download_workflowhub.py
│   ├── index.json              all workflow IDs
│   ├── meta/<id>.json          metadata per workflow (all types)
│   ├── crates/                 RO-Crate zips (Galaxy only)
│   ├── ga/<id>_v<version>/     .ga files from the crates
│   └── workflows.csv           one row per workflow
├── galaxy/                     galaxy_tool_pairs.py, ToolShed names
│   ├── galaxy_pairs.csv        tool A feeds tool B, per workflow
│   ├── galaxy_edges.csv        same pairs, counted over workflows
│   └── galaxy_tools.csv        tools and how many workflows use them
├── mapping/                    ToolShed name -> bio.tools ID
│   ├── galaxy_codex_tools.tsv  Galaxy Codex table (downloaded)
│   └── galaxy_tool_map.csv     the mapping, with method used
└── biotools/                   map_galaxy_tools.py, bio.tools IDs
    ├── biotools_pairs.csv      per workflow
    ├── biotools_edges.csv      edges, to compare with the literature graph
    └── biotools_nodes.csv      node attributes (ToolShed names, n_workflows)
```

Main output is the `data/biotools/biotools_edges.csv` which has aggregated connection weight between any pair of tools and cna be used to 
compare with the literature graph.
Each tools mapping method is noted in `galaxy_tool_map.csv`. The data should probably be grouped by confidence in match; 
codex > name match > prefix name match.
An edge is as reliable as its weaker end, so the comparison can be run on codex-only, codex + name, and all edges to check if weaker 
mappings change the result.